--- a/man/aptly.1.ronn.tmpl
+++ b/man/aptly.1.ronn.tmpl
@@ -557,6 +557,14 @@ Examples:
     matches all packages that provide `mail-transport` with name that has no suffix `-dev` and
     with version greater or equal to `3.5`.
 
+Queries starting with `?` or `~` are apt patterns (see apt-patterns(7)), e.g. `?depends(?virtual)` or
+`~nlib ~ri386`. A pattern selects the binary packages `apt list --all-versions` would list for it, with the
+queried packages as the only source, `amd64` as the native architecture and nothing installed: every version of
+a matching package (name and architecture, packages of architecture `all` belong to `amd64`). Source packages
+are never selected. As in apt, the arguments of `?name`, `?section`, `?source-package`, `?source-version` and
+`?version` are POSIX extended regular expressions matched case-insensitively anywhere in the string, while
+`?exact-name` and `?priority` compare names exactly.
+
 When specified on command line, query may have to be quoted according to shell rules, so that it stays single argument:
 
